                <h2>Quick Answer: Two Very Different Types of “No Credit Check Loans”</h2>
                <p>When Americans Google “no credit check loans,” they actually mean two opposite things. Regulators like the CFPB treat them completely differently:</p>
                <div class="not-prose my-6 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="rounded-[16px] border-2 border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50/50 p-5">
                        <div class="text-[13px] font-extrabold text-emerald-700">✅ CREDIT-BUILDER LOANS</div>
                        <div class="mt-1 font-bold">That Actually Build Credit</div>
                        <ul class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-700 list-disc pl-4 space-y-1">
                            <li>Reports payments to 1-3 bureaus</li>
                            <li>APR 0% - 15.99% or small admin fee</li>
                            <li>Holds money in locked savings/CD</li>
                            <li>No hard inquiry or soft-only check</li>
                            <li>Goal: Build history, not trap you</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rounded-[16px] border-2 border-red-200 bg-red-50/50 p-5">
                        <div class="text-[13px] font-extrabold text-red-700">🚫 PREDATORY PAYDAY / TITLE</div>
                        <div class="mt-1 font-bold">The CFPB Warning Zone</div>
                        <ul class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-700 list-disc pl-4 space-y-1">
                            <li>Does NOT report on-time payments, only collections</li>
                            <li>APR 300% - 700% + rollover fees</li>
                            <li>Requires bank access, post-dated check, car title</li>
                            <li>No underwriting ability-to-repay</li>
                            <li>Goal: Rollover cycle — CFPB says 80% roll over</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2>3 No Credit Check Loans That DO Build Credit in 2026 (US)</h2>
                <p>According to <a href="https://www.experian.com/blogs/ask-experian/credit-builder-loans/" class="text-emerald-600 underline">Experian</a> and Equifax, a credit-builder installment account can help if it reports as an installment loan and you pay on time. These three US options meet the bar:</p>

                <h3>1. Self Credit Builder Loan — The Classic</h3>
                <p><strong>How it works:</strong> You choose a term (12-24 months) and amount ($25-$150/mo). Self holds funds in a CD at Lead Bank. No hard credit pull. Payment history reported to all 3 — Experian, Equifax, TransUnion, per <a href="https://www.self.inc/" class="text-emerald-600 underline">Self disclosure</a>.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Credit check:</strong> No hard pull. Soft check / bank verification only. No SSN required for eligibility check? SSN required at funding for CD per Patriot Act, but no score needed.</li>
                    <li><strong>APR / Fees:</strong> ~15.92% APR + one-time $9 admin fee (sample). Not 0%, but far from predatory. You get money back minus interest at end.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reporting:</strong> All 3 bureaus. Builds payment history (35% of FICO).</li>
                    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> True 0 credit file in the US.</li>
                    <li><strong>Watch out:</strong> Late payments reported too. Fees for late.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>2. MoneyLion Credit Builder Plus (Instacash + Membership)</h3>
                <p>MoneyLion offers Credit Builder Plus up to $1,000, no credit check, no hard inquiry. Membership $19.99/mo includes credit tracking. Per MoneyLion terms, up to 50% of loan reports to all 3 bureaus via TransUnion, Equifax, Experian.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Credit check:</strong> No credit check to join. Bank account history used.</li>
                    <li><strong>APR / Fees:</strong> 0% APR on credit builder portion + membership fee. Cheaper than payday if you need the suite.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reporting:</strong> All 3 bureaus (portion of account).</li>
                    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Need $500 quickly AND want it reported.</li>
                    <li><strong>Watch out:</strong> Membership cost means you pay even if you don’t borrow. Cancel if not using. Check <a href="https://www.equifax.com/personal/education/credit-score/how-credit-builder-loans-work/" class="underline text-emerald-600">Equifax guidance</a> on membership loans.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>3. Possible Finance — Pay in 4 Installment That Reports</h3>
                <p>Possible offers up to $500 in 4 bi-weekly payments, reports to TransUnion, Equifax, and Experian. No FICO check, uses bank data. Claims no rollover trap vs payday.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Credit check:</strong> No hard or soft credit score pull. Bank account verification.</li>
                    <li><strong>APR / Fees:</strong> 0% APR example + fee? State dependent: ~$15-$30 per $100 borrowed but reported as installment, capped. Up to 150% APR equivalent in some states — still high vs Self, but no rollover per Possible terms, unlike payday.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reporting:</strong> Reports to all 3 if you repay on time; also reports late.</li>
                    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Short-term cash need in CA, TX, FL, OH, WA where available; better than payday storefront.</li>
                    <li><strong>Watch out:</strong> Not available in all 50 states. State disclosure required.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-[16px] border border-slate-200 overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white px-5 py-3 text-[13px] font-bold">Comparison Table: Builder vs Predatory (US 2026)</div>
