                <h2>CFPB Warnings: What Makes a Loan Predatory? (2026 Update)</h2>
                <p>The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/en-1567/what-is-a-payday-loan/" class="text-emerald-600 underline">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB)</a> defines predatory payday lending with these red flags. If you see 2+ of these, walk away:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>No ability-to-repay assessment:</strong> Lender doesn’t ask income, expenses, bank balance. Required under CFPB Payday Rule rescinded partially but still best practice.</li>
                    <li><strong>APR above 36%:</strong> CFPB and Military Lending Act use 36% as high-cost threshold. Payday often 391% APR for $15 fee on $100 for 14 days.</li>
                    <li><strong>Direct access to bank account or title:</strong> Requires ACH authorization or car title collateral. CFPB data: 20% of auto title borrowers lose car.</li>
                    <li><strong>Rollover / re-borrowing:</strong> Lender encourages renewal instead of repayment. CFPB found average borrower in debt 5 months of year.</li>
                    <li><strong>Doesn’t report positive history:</strong> Only reports if you default to collection, never your on-time payments to Equifax/Experian/TransUnion.</li>
                    <li><strong>Hidden fees — “tips” and “donations”:</strong> Cash advance apps disguising fees as optional tips that function as interest. CFPB is scrutinizing in 2025-2026.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>What to do if trapped:</strong> Contact your state AG, file complaint at consumerfinance.gov/complaint, ask bank to revoke ACH, and seek non-profit credit counselor NFCC.org.</p>

                <h2>APR Traps: How to Calculate the Real Cost</h2>
                <p>Let’s do math the CFPB uses to show trap size, US focused:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Example payday:</strong> Borrow $500, fee $75 for 2 weeks = if rolled 8 times (4 months), pay $600 in fees, still owe $500. Effective APR = (75/500)*26 = 390%.</li>
                    <li><strong>Example Self:</strong> $25/mo x 12 = $300 total, $9 admin + interest ~ $28, you get ~$263 back. Net cost ~ $37 for 12 months reporting.</li>
                    <li><strong>Possible vs payday:</strong> $200 in 4 payments = ~$30 fee = ~197% APR equivalent but capped vs unlimited rollover.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Rule:</strong> If APR >36% and no bureau reporting, it’s not building credit — it’s building debt. Use <a href="https://www.equifax.com/personal/education/credit-score/articles/-/learn/how-to-build-credit/" class="text-emerald-600 underline">Equifax’s build credit guide</a> to choose reporting product instead.</p>

                <h2>What to Avoid with No Credit Check Offers</h2>
                <div class="not-prose space-y-3 my-4">
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-white p-4 text-[13.5px] leading-6"><span class="font-bold text-red-700">1. Title loans:</span> Lose car if 1 payment late. CFPB: 1 in 5 repossessed. APR 300%. No credit benefit per TransUnion.</div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-white p-4 text-[13.5px] leading-6"><span class="font-bold text-red-700">2. Rent-to-own ads as credit:</span> Doesn’t report to Equifax unless goes to collections.</div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-white p-4 text-[13.5px] leading-6"><span class="font-bold text-red-700">3. “No SSN” guaranteed loans:</span> Legit US banks require SSN/TIN per Patriot Act for CD/installment. No-SSN + guaranteed = scam indicator. (We note: No SSN needed to compare, but needed to fund).</div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-white p-4 text-[13.5px] leading-6"><span class="font-bold text-red-700">4. Upfront fee loans:</span> CFPB advance-fee scam warning: Legit lender never asks fee before funding.</div>
                </div>

                <h2>Better Alternatives in the US in 2026</h2>
                <p>If your goal is build from 0 to 700, consider these before any no-credit-check cash loan:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Secured card that reports to 3 bureaus</strong> — Discover, Capital One Platinum Secured. Utilization <30% rule from Experian.</li>
                    <li><strong>Chime Credit Builder Visa® Secured</strong> — No credit check, reports as secured card, no interest.</li>
                    <li><strong>Become authorized user</strong> — Family card, ensures it reports to Equifax/Experian per card issuer.</li>
                    <li><strong>Credit Builder + Free Score Tracker</strong> — ScoreUp’s free calculator estimates impact of adding installment account vs revolving mix (10% of FICO).</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-[16px] bg-slate-900 text-white p-6 flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-5 items-start justify-between">
                    <div><div class="text-[18px] font-bold leading-tight">Ready to build credit without payday debt?</div><p class="mt-1.5 text-[13px] text-slate-400 leading-6 max-w-[420px]">Compare credit cards for no credit history and run free score simulator — no SSN, no email needed. Based on Experian FICO® factors.</p></div>
                    <div class="flex gap-2 shrink-0"><a href="/Pages/CreditCards" class="h-11 px-6 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 text-white font-semibold text-[14px] flex items-center justify-center hover:bg-emerald-600">Compare Cards →</a><a href="/#calculators" class="h-11 px-6 rounded-full bg-white text-slate-900 font-semibold text-[14px] flex items-center justify-center">Calculator</a></div>
                </div>

                <h2>FAQs — No Credit Check Loans & Credit Building</h2>
                <div class="not-prose divide-y divide-slate-200 border border-slate-200 rounded-[16px] overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[14px]">Do no credit check loans build credit?</div><p class="mt-1.5 text-[13.5px] text-slate-600 leading-6">Only if they report to Experian, Equifax, TransUnion as installment or credit-builder account. Most payday, title, and cash advance no-credit-check loans do not report on-time payments, so they do not build credit per CFPB and Experian. Self, MoneyLion Credit Builder Plus, and Possible do report.</p></div>
                    <div class="p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[14px]">What loan can I get with no credit check and no SSN in the US?</div><p class="mt-1.5 text-[13.5px] text-slate-600 leading-6">You can compare without SSN, but to open a federally insured CD/installment in US, you need SSN/ITIN for identity verification under US law. Be wary of sites advertising guaranteed no-SSN loans — often advance-fee scams flagged by CFPB.</p></div>
                    <div class="p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[14px]">Which is better: Self vs MoneyLion vs Possible for 0 credit?</div><p class="mt-1.5 text-[13.5px] text-slate-600 leading-6">For pure building from zero with lowest cost: Self. For need small cash now + reporting: Possible in available states. For bundle of banking + credit building: MoneyLion. All report to 3 bureaus; none require FICO.</p></div>
                    <div class="p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[14px]">Why does CFPB warn about payday loans?</div><p class="mt-1.5 text-[13.5px] text-slate-600 leading-6">Per <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/data-research/research-reports/cfpb-payday-lending-rule/" class="underline">CFPB research</a>, 80% of payday loans are rolled over or followed by another within 14 days, average borrower pays more in fees than principal. APR often 391%+.</p></div>
                    <div class="p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[14px]">Does Experian Boost count?</div><p class="mt-1.5 text-[13.5px] text-slate-600 leading-6">Experian Boost reports rent/utilities you already pay, may raise Experian score only, not Equifax/TransUnion. Useful addition but not replacement for installment history.</p></div>
